The '''Ballade No. 4 in F minor''', Op. 52 is a [[Ballades (Chopin)|ballade]] for solo piano by [[Frédéric Chopin]], completed in 1842 in [[Paris]] and published in 1843 with a dedication to [[Charlotte de Rothschild|Baroness Charlotte de Rothschild]].{{sfn|Jonson|1905|p = 157}} Being his last published [[Ballades (Chopin)|ballade]], the piece is commonly considered one of the masterpieces of 19th-century piano music.{{sfn|Samson|1994|p=115}}{{sfn|Walker|2018|p = 449}}

Of the four ballades, it is considered by many pianists to be the most difficult, both technically and musically.<ref name="ourchopin" /><ref name="Huneker" /> It is also the longest, taking around ten to twelve minutes to perform. According to [[John Ogdon]], it is "the most exalted, intense and sublimely powerful of all Chopin's compositions... It is unbelievable that it lasts only twelve minutes, for it contains the experience of a lifetime."<ref>{{cite web| title=Chopin Music: Ballades| url=http://www.chopinmusic.net/works/ballades| date=2009-06-13| access-date=2010-01-06| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100127033624/http://www.chopinmusic.net/works/ballades| archive-date=27 January 2010| url-status=dead}}</ref>

== History ==
The circumstances of composition are poorly documented, though it appears that Chopin began composing shortly after the completion of [[Ballade No. 3 (Chopin)|Ballade No. 3]]. By December 1842, the ballade was finished, and he offered it for sale to [[Breitkopf & Härtel]], along with the [[Polonaise in A-flat major, Op. 53|''Heroic'' Polonaise]] and the [[Scherzo No. 4 (Chopin)|fourth Scherzo]].<ref name="Henle">{{Cite book|last=Müllemann|first=Norbert|url=https://www.henle.de/en/detail/?Title=Balladen_862|title=Chopin Ballades – Preface|publisher=[[G. Henle Verlag]]|year=2007|location=Munich|pages=IX-XIII}}</ref>

The work was dedicated to [[Charlotte de Rothschild|Baroness Charlotte de Rothschild]], wife of Nathaniel de Rothschild,<ref name="Huneker">{{cite book|title=Chopin: the Man and his Music|last=Huneker|first=James|author-link=James Huneker|year=1921|isbn=1-60303-588-5|page=414|publisher=Plain Label Books |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=oxUogRL4n2kC|access-date=2010-01-05}}</ref> who had invited Chopin to play in her [[Paris|Parisian]] residence, where she introduced him to the aristocracy and nobility.

In the preface to his edition of Chopin's ballades, [[Alfred Cortot]] claims that the inspiration for this ballade is [[Adam Mickiewicz]]'s poem ''The Three Budrys'', which tells of three brothers sent away by their father to seek treasures, and the story of their return with three Polish brides.<ref>Foreword, ''The Ballads of Chopin'', Salabert Editions. An English version of the poem can be found [http://daisy.htmlplanet.com/budrys.html here] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100719033845/http://daisy.htmlplanet.com/budrys.html |date=2010-07-19 }}</ref>

== Structure ==
A phrase in C major (marked ''piano'') opens the seven introductory bars and leads into the first subject of sonata-form exposition.{{sfn|Samson|1994|p=112}} The first theme undergoes four cumulative transformations with decorations, [[Counter-melody|counter-melodies]], and a [[nocturne]]-like [[fioritura]].{{sfn|Samson|1994|p = 115}} The development of the second theme and its intertwining with the first heightens the complexity of the musical structure and builds tension. Through the intertwining and thus the simultaneous development of the two themes, Chopin effectively combines the use of both the [[sonata form]] and the [[Variation (music)|variation form]].<ref name="howto">[http://www.chopin.org/lessons/How%20to%20Play%20Chopin%20PART%20V%20-%20Chopins_BALLADES.pdf How to Play Chopin: Chopin's Ballades] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140315131023/http://www.chopin.org/lessons/How%20to%20Play%20Chopin%20PART%20V%20-%20Chopins_BALLADES.pdf |date=15 March 2014 }}, Regina Smendzianka</ref> The body of the piece concludes with a series of accented ''fortissimo'' chords, followed by a momentary calm of five ''pianissimo'' chords.{{sfn|Walker|1973|p = 58}} This then suddenly leads into an extremely fast, turbulent [[coda (music)|coda]], written in exuberant [[counterpoint]]. Structurally, Ballade No. 4 is decidedly intricate.<ref name="ourchopin">{{Cite web|title=Chopin: Complete Music Analysis – Ballades|url=http://www.ourchopin.com/analysis/ballade.html|access-date=2020-12-17|website=ourchopin.com}}</ref><ref name="Huneker" />

=== Analysis ===
